UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "exec_id" = 100, "notes" = 'IT MAY ANSWER TABLE 13 FOOTNOTE c, AND THE COINCIDENCE IS STRIKING RATHER THAN CONCLUSIVE. Steinfeld''s Table 13 declares four allowable bands -- 1:8, 1:10, 1:12, 1:16 -- and marks the first two ''Based on research of others (Templer, 1977 and Walters, 1971)''. Templer''s Table 20 makes recommendations over THE SAME FOUR GRADIENTS. NOT GUESSED, per batch 18 section 9 item 3: Volume 3 is dated May 1980 and footnote c says 1977, so this cannot be the cited item as printed; the contract''s field tests ran Jul 1976 to Sep 1977, which makes an INTERIM REPORT of the same project a plausible referent, and that is a hypothesis to test by retrieval rather than a conclusion. IT ALSO CARRIES WALTER 1971 SECOND-HAND, which bears on GAP-026: its literature review describes Walter''s adjustable laboratory ramp in detail -- 20 feet at gradients 1:8.6 to 1:16, 10 feet at 1:5.5 to 1:10, four-point rating scale -- so a document this project CAN retrieve reports the study it cannot. That is corroboration of Steinfeld''s account of Walter by an independent reader, not a substitute for Walter itself. AND IT CONTESTS CANDIDATE 110''s AUTHOR: its reference list cites the 1957 Illinois dissertation as ''Dixon, Charles E.'', where Steinfeld''s bibliography plainly prints ''Elmer, C.D.''. Two primary sources disagree; neither is corrected from the other. RETRIEVAL IS FREE AND THE ROUTE IS PROVEN: archive.org/download/provisionsforeld00temp_0/provisionsforeld00temp_0_djvu.txt. Unlike Walter 1971 and Elmer 1957 this needs no document delivery. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source; admitted as REF-01008): ADMITTED AS REF-01008, T3 GREY (owner ruling 2026-09-18: pre-1990 work is historical grounding, the REF-01005 treatment). Scan PDF (db21e48738335d82.pdf, 334 images) and DjVuTXT persisted from Internet Archive provisionsforeld00temp_0 (CC BY-NC-ND 3.0, National Transportation Library); every table extracted was RENDERED AND READ, not taken from the text layer. R15 CORRECTIONS TO THE STAGED ROW: (1) AUTHOR. Staged as ''Templer, John A. et al.''; the Technical Report Documentation Page (rendered, index 2) names ONE author, John A. Templer -- the research associates and assistants are credited as the project team, not as authors. Filed as a sole author. (2) WHAT TABLE 20 IS. Staged as ''a recommend/not-recommend matrix over curb heights 3, 6 and 9 inches against gradients 1:8, 1:10, 1:12 and 1:16'', which is right, and it has a consequence the staged row did not draw: it is a CURB-RAMP table whose recommendation is RISE-CONDITIONED -- 1:8 up to a 3-inch curb, 1:10 up to 6 inches, 1:12 up to 9 inches, steeper than 1:8 never, with the footnote ''Whenever possible slopes less than the maximum should be employed'' -- and its own conclusion is ''steeper ramps are acceptable if they are short''. Two of its cells (1:10 at 6 in, 1:12 at 9 in) were not tested; they interpolate between tested ramps. (3) THE SAMPLE. 120 volunteers, August-November 1976, in eight groups; the 18 manual wheelchair users are young (at least 16 aged 16-35). (4) THE INSTRUMENT. A four-point subject difficulty rating plus an independent tester rating; heart rate and oxygen consumption were considered and rejected. WHAT STANDS: the Walter 1971 account at second hand (printed p.4) -- now extraction 64 -- and the ''Dixon, Charles E.'' attribution of the 1957 dissertation (reference 1), which keeps candidate 110''s author contest open. FOOTNOTE c: this report''s test ramps 9 (1:8, 2 ft, 3 in) and 6 (1:10.67, 8 ft, 9 in) have EXACTLY the run and rise of REF-01005 Table 13''s two footnote-c rows, and ramp 6 was unacceptable to manual wheelchair users in ascent (extraction 66). That is consistent with footnote c''s ''Templer, 1977'' being this project''s ramp work, NOT an identification: this volume is dated May 1980. Candidate 115 stays PENDING. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source; admitted as REF-01008): CORRECTION, SAME BATCH (independent adversarial pass, finding #21): ''334 images'' above is Internet Archive''s imagecount metadata. The persisted PDF (db21e48738335d82.pdf) has 332 pages, and every page index batch 20 cites is a zero-based index into that PDF. ALSO: the untested Table 20 cells are the report''s recommendations, not measurements -- ''interpolate'' above is batch 20''s inference, not the source''s word (see extractions 61 and 62).

